# API Conventions

## Router Organisation

One router file per bounded context. NEVER put all routes in a single file.
Target routers:
- `programmes.py` -- programme CRUD and configuration
- `pipelines.py` -- pipeline definitions, trigger runs
- `jobs.py` -- job submission, status, cancellation
- `episodes.py` -- clinical episode queries
- `encounters.py` -- encounter coding, review, labels
- `quality.py` -- quality issues, guideline alerts
- `terminology.py` -- ValueSets, ConceptMaps, UMLS
- `audit.py` -- audit trail, change history

Each router file <=300 LOC. Split further if needed.

## Programme Scoping

- All clinical endpoints are scoped to a programme: `/api/v1/programmes/{programme_id}/...`
- Programme context is resolved via path parameter and injected as a dependency.
- Cross-programme endpoints (e.g., patient lookup) live under `/api/v1/patients/...`.

## Async Job Pattern (Fire-and-Track)

Long-running operations use this contract:

```
POST /api/v1/jobs                        -> 202 {job_id, status: "queued"}
GET  /api/v1/jobs/:id                    -> 200 {job_id, status, progress, steps, result}
GET  /api/v1/jobs/:id/steps              -> 200 [{step_name, capability, status, duration}]
POST /api/v1/jobs/:id/cancel             -> 200 {status: "cancelled"}
```

- The API enqueues work to Redis Streams and returns 202 immediately.
- A separate worker process consumes from the stream and executes pipeline steps.
- Job status is polled from Redis (fast) with full detail from Neo4j (on demand).
- Workers update both Redis (status hash) and Neo4j (Job/JobStep nodes).

## Pipeline Trigger Endpoints

```
POST /api/v1/programmes/{prog}/pipelines/{pipeline}/run
  body: {patient_id, organization_id, domains?, date_range?, params?}
  -> 202 {job_id}
```

Different pipelines for different use cases (all configured in Neo4j):
- `full-audit` -- complete pathway reconstruction
- `quick-code` -- code encounters only
- `screen-and-alert` -- code + abnormals + guideline check, branch if needed

## Request / Response Models

- Pydantic v2 for ALL request and response models.
- Models in `models/` directory, one file per domain.
- Use `Field(...)` with descriptions for API docs.
- Dates as ISO 8601 strings. UUIDs as strings.

## Error Handling

- Structured errors: `{"error": {"code": "...", "message": "...", "detail": ...}}`.
- FastAPI exception handlers. Never return raw stack traces.
- Correlation ID (`trace_id` / `job_id`) on all error logs.

## Authentication

- Azure AD JWT validation on all endpoints via middleware.
- Role checks via dependency injection: `Depends(require_role("ai.reviewer"))`.
- 401 for unauthenticated. 403 for insufficient role.

## Versioning

- API prefix: `/api/v1/`. Increment major version for breaking changes only.
